WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:07.000
Hi, everybody.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.570
My name is Hannah Podzorski.

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:10.450
And today I want to
talk to you about how

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:13.510
I adapted current tools
in environmental science

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:17.110
through R by advocating
for automation.

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:19.000
And to start, I want to
tell you a little bit

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.550
about my colleague,

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.900
Let's call him John.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:24.490
I work with a lot of John's.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:29.360
John might be a biologist, a
chemist, maybe a geologist.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.480
He's been in his field
for quite some time,

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.330
and he has a lot of very
important technical knowledge

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:37.600
within his field, but little
to no knowledge on modern day

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.780
programming languages.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.810
In addition to this, John
keeps getting handed data

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:46.530
sets that are much larger
and more complex than what

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:49.440
his current tools can handle.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.360
And on top of this, just
like everybody else,

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.540
he's got too many tasks
and not enough time.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.400
So this is the type
of scenario where I

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.440
would advocate for automation.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.880
My goal is to streamline
the processing of data,

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:04.110
so my team has more time to
apply their technical knowledge

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:07.420
to the problem at hand.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.360
But John might be a
little bit hesitant,

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:14.530
and I understand that
because as a programmer,

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:17.530
I would much rather spend
five days automating something

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:20.573
than 5 minutes to
do a simple task.

00:01:20.573 --> 00:01:22.240
And because of that,
not only today

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.282
am I going to talk
to you about how

00:01:24.282 --> 00:01:26.650
I used R to automate
workflows, but also how

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:28.600
I reduced the
activation energy it

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.550
took to get my team on board.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.770
So in chemistry,
activation energy

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:37.680
is the energy needed to get from
one side of a chemical reaction

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.750
to another.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.760
And you can reduce
the activation energy

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.400
by applying a catalyst.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.010
So when I want to move my team
from their current workflow

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:50.850
to an automated workflow, I can
reduce the activation energy

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:53.490
by rephrasing how
I pitch automation,

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:58.080
starting small, and being
a little bit creative.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.720
So let's start with the
pitch and most importantly,

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.390
that word automation.

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:05.050
It can be a little vague
and feel dependent.

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:07.050
But what I mean when
I say automation

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:09.780
is the streamlining of
a workflow by reducing

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.460
unnecessary human interactions.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:17.670
So my team has more time to
spend on greater value tasks.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.500
And most times when
I pitch automation,

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.102
I don't even use
the word automation.

00:02:21.102 --> 00:02:22.685
I say, "Let's streamline
this workflow!"

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.452
So let's talk about the
difference between workflow

00:02:27.452 --> 00:02:28.910
that I traditionally
see, which I'm

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:31.202
going to call for the purposes
of this talk, reactionary

00:02:31.202 --> 00:02:33.740
workflow and automated workflow.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:37.760
So let's say we have 4
work products A, B, C and D,

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.430
A might be our
database, B might be

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:45.470
our tables full of summary stats,
C is our figures, and D is

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:47.960
the text that goes
into our final report.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:50.355
Say we have to
update our database.

00:02:50.355 --> 00:02:52.730
Maybe we know that we need to
update our database, like we

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:56.060
need to go out and collect more
data, or maybe we don't know

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:59.510
and maybe someone found an
error in unit conversions

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:01.170
at the last minute.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.000
Well, with this
change now we have

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.170
to manually update all of
the workflow downstream,

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:10.920
where in comparison, in an
automated workflow, if you have

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.020
to make that one change
to your database,

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.210
you can use functions
or scripts to automate

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:18.390
the processing of that
one edit and apply it

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:20.370
to all of your work
products downstream.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.920
And this is, I know, a
very simple definition

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:26.010
of what automation is and
streamlining of workflow,

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:27.450
but I like to lay
it out like this

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:31.290
because it makes the pros or
benefits of using automation

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:32.850
very obvious.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.940
So number one, reproducibility,
because of those scripts

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:39.270
or those recipes that are
being completed by a computer,

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:42.160
you know, you're going to get
the same thing every time.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.770
And this is important not
just when you can predict

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:47.070
you need to update
something, like we're

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:48.960
going to do annual
sampling events,

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:50.967
but also when you
can't predict you're

00:03:50.967 --> 00:03:52.800
going to need to have
to update something,

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.330
like when you catch an error in
a simple mathematic equation.

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:59.275
Also, it can be
extremely simple,

00:03:59.275 --> 00:04:01.650
and though it pains me to say
this, this type of workflow

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:04.290
can be accomplished
in Excel workbooks,

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:07.200
just maybe not at the
current scale we need.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.780
It saves time because you're
reducing that human interaction

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.880
and also reducing
the amount of errors,

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:15.030
which saves my team time in
quality control and quality

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:17.050
assurance.

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:19.930
So I found it helpful
to start small

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:22.750
with tasks that can be automated
in the same amount of time

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:24.310
as the original tasks.

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:26.440
And so for me and the
projects I work on,

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:28.840
this is most likely
going to be moving

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:31.390
tables of summary
statistics and figures out

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:34.130
of Excel and into R.

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:37.220
But most importantly, I like
to try and meet my team members

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:39.710
where they are, and that's
going to be by creating work

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:41.280
products they're familiar with.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.790
And at least in my case,
that's going to most likely

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:46.510
be Microsoft Office products.

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:49.390
So I'm going to talk about two
packages I love for doing this,

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:52.190
the first one being openxlsx.

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:56.110
It's a package designed
to edit, read, and create

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:58.740
Excel files in R.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:02.880
So traditionally when I go
to save a dataframe in R,

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.660
I'm going to write it as a CSV.

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:06.660
However, more
recently, I've tried

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:09.180
to force myself to
save more of these data

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:10.740
frames, especially
the ones I know

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:14.737
that are going to be of interest
to my team, to Excel files.

00:05:14.737 --> 00:05:16.320
And that's because
I want to help them

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.770
with this transition between
the reactionary workflow

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:21.880
and the automated workflow.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:24.120
Because when they're
working on so many projects

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:27.240
at the same time, and they're
used to all their files

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:30.240
being dynamic and
constantly changing,

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.950
and also all written in excel,
and you throw one CSV in there,

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:36.220
more than once

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:38.230
have they opened
that CSV in excel,

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:40.860
added a few tabs,
highlight a few cells,

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:42.670
saved it, and closed out.

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:45.390
And since those edits
are not supported in CSV,

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:46.950
they just lost all that work.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:49.890
So by me making the simple
change of simply saving

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:53.820
data frames as .xls
files, I'm helping

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:57.090
to reduce the activation energy
and get my team moving forward

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:58.440
towards automation.

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:01.350
Also, I love openxlsx
because it allows

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:04.210
me to make formatted Excel
tables in R such as the one

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:05.103
that you see here.

00:06:05.103 --> 00:06:07.020
And it's great for when
I have to put together

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:10.380
hundreds of tables that will
go into the back

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:12.600
of a report, which I'm
sure eventually one day

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:17.070
someone might look at, maybe.

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:18.370
Also, I love to use

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:21.630
officer. officer allows you
to manipulate word documents

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:24.330
and PowerPoint
documents in R. I use it

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:27.700
mostly for exporting
ggplots into PowerPoint.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:30.900
And so here's just example
code doing just that.

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:33.720
But what I want to do is I
want to point out the rvg

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:36.550
package and the function dml.

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:39.130
So running your ggplot
object through this--

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:42.390
in our case, our ggplot object is
"plot"--through this function

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:45.630
before you export that
figure to PowerPoint

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:48.820
allows you to actually edit
that figure in PowerPoint.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:52.920
So here's an example of a
plot I created and exported

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.950
to PowerPoint using the code
from the previous slide.

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:59.640
It's the concentration
of chloride over time

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:02.580
and since I use
the dml function,

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:06.760
I can move around my
annotations, change them,

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:09.813
I can change the axis
titles and the legend.

00:07:09.813 --> 00:07:12.480
And this is great for if my team
wants to just have a little bit

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:15.330
more control of
the final product

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:18.240
or if they just don't like where
the annotation is currently at,

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.610
or if they want to make
last minute nomenclature

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:24.130
adjustments, this makes it
great so we can still interact.

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:27.030
In addition, with officer, you
can use PowerPoint templates

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:29.610
to create placeholders,
and then you

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:32.940
can add things like figure
captions, figure numbers,

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:34.390
notes, logos.

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:37.470
So I can automate the process
of creating these figures

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:39.885
and export them
from R into PowerPoint

00:07:39.885 --> 00:07:41.135
so they're ready for a report.

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:49.280
So now that I've got my
team a little bit on board,

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:51.240
I've started with
these smaller tasks.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:52.910
Now I can take on
bigger tasks that

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:57.380
might require a little bit
more of an initial investment

00:07:57.380 --> 00:07:59.570
and delayed gratification.

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:02.900
So I'm going to talk just about
that, a specific example where

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:05.690
my team decided to
automate software developed

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:08.050
by a regulatory agency.

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:10.490
So that software is ProUCL.

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:13.390
ProUCL was developed by the
US Environmental Protection

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:16.360
Agency, or EPA,
and it specializes

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:20.410
in performing statistical
analysis on Left Censored data.

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:21.910
In Environmental
Sciences, we have

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:23.390
a lot of left censored data.

00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:26.560
We, whenever we're measuring the
concentration of some compound

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:29.080
in water or soil
or air, depending

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:31.870
on our methods and our
equipment, we have something

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:33.020
called a detection limit.

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:34.570
And below that
detection limit, we

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:36.890
don't know where that
concentration will sit.

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:39.409
It can be anywhere between
0 and our detection limit.

00:08:39.409 --> 00:08:42.159
So there's a lot of
uncertainty around that.

00:08:42.159 --> 00:08:44.560
So ProUCL takes that
uncertainty into account

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:47.440
when doing the statistics,
when doing simple statistics

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:49.180
like means and
standard deviations,

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:51.880
as well as more complex
statistics like distributions

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:54.610
and upper confidence limits.

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:58.660
So ProUCL is definitely, it's
solving a problem for us,

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:03.460
but it has some less than great
design features, one of which

00:09:03.460 --> 00:09:06.760
is it will crash if you
put too much data into it.

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:09.130
And by too much I mean
about 10,000 records,

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:11.590
which for the type of
projects we're working on,

00:09:11.590 --> 00:09:13.510
which require us
to analyze hundreds

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:17.270
of compounds in a variety
of environmental media,

00:09:17.270 --> 00:09:20.478
we were having to run
ProUCL multiple times,

00:09:20.478 --> 00:09:23.020
and because of that, we decided
we wanted to try and automate

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:29.080
it, and we decided to try and
automate it by using a mini--

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:30.260
a mouse macro.

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:33.400
So in our case, it's
called a mini mouse macro

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:36.130
and basically automate the
clicking of the software

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:37.580
itself.

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:42.050
So we wrote a function
in R that allows

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:46.190
us to subset that data into
more manageable chunks.

00:09:46.190 --> 00:09:48.320
And then you run
the function and it

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:51.020
creates an input file
for our mouse macro

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:53.430
and then it runs that file.

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:55.990
So the mouse macro input
is just a simple text file.

00:09:55.990 --> 00:09:59.420
I use some command line code
to open up the mouse macro

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:02.000
and load in the
input file and then

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:05.322
it just clicks through
the software for you.

00:10:05.322 --> 00:10:06.780
So here it's using
the text string

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:10.170
we gave it to input the
location of the input file.

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:14.120
It's clicking through all
of the dropdowns we'll need

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:17.540
and then it's loading in the
compounds we want to look at.

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:20.340
And finally, it'll save
that file to the text string

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:22.990
I gave it for the output file.

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:25.930
And then once it senses
that there is information

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:28.810
in that file, it will close out
of ProUCL, and if necessary,

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:29.750
run it again.

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:31.760
So we'll loop this over
and over and over again.

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:33.950
And that might have seemed like
a very simple process.

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:35.090
Why would you automate it?

00:10:35.090 --> 00:10:37.360
Well, that was a
very small data set,

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:39.550
and there's actually a
lot of computation time

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:42.740
that has to go into it when
you're doing larger data sets.

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:45.460
So for small projects,
running ProUCL

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:49.420
through the automation like
this can take 15 to 30 minutes.

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:51.400
But for bigger projects,
we're talking just

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:54.520
automating like
this 6 to 8 hours.

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:57.190
So it's definitely
saving us time that way.

00:10:57.190 --> 00:10:59.770
But writing the code to
automate the clicking of my mouse

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:02.140
through the software was
actually the easy part.

00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:07.120
The hard part was
deciphering the output file.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:10.730
So here's what ProUCL
gives you as an outfit file.

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:13.060
It's a formatted Excel sheet.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:16.000
This is just a small subsection
of the statistics it gives

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:17.572
you and only one compound.

00:11:17.572 --> 00:11:19.030
So once again, for
our sites, we're

00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:21.910
looking at hundreds of
compounds and we might even have

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:23.420
to group our data spatially,

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:27.790
so we will need
additional information.

00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:30.580
And so before we automated,
what we did was just

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:32.350
have someone go through
and copy and paste

00:11:32.350 --> 00:11:35.200
the values we were
really interested in.

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:37.220
And that took quite some time.

00:11:37.220 --> 00:11:40.000
So instead, we
automated it and now we

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:42.280
create tables both
unformatted and formatted

00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:44.740
in Excel that my team can
use for their analysis

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:45.945
and their final report.

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:52.910
So overall, my team
thinks this went great.

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:55.400
It does exactly what
we want it to do.

00:11:55.400 --> 00:11:58.160
When we first decided we
wanted to automate ProUCL,

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:00.020
we saw two paths
we could go down,

00:12:00.020 --> 00:12:03.920
one using our mouse macro,
the other just recreating all

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:06.290
of the statistical tests in R.

00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:08.660
And that definitely
would have been possible,

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:12.050
but at the time we just didn't
have the resources or the time

00:12:12.050 --> 00:12:13.920
to devote to such a project.

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:18.710
In addition, ProUCL
is an industry standard almost

00:12:18.710 --> 00:12:19.500
at this point.

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:20.960
It's been used for
quite some time

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:23.300
and there's a lot of important
technical knowledge built

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:24.180
into it.

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:27.050
So using it is a benefit,
especially when we

00:12:27.050 --> 00:12:29.300
have to deal with regulators.

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:32.270
In addition, ProUCL
is used on projects

00:12:32.270 --> 00:12:33.950
such as risk
assessments where we

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:35.990
look at how compounds
in the environment

00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:40.040
can affect different receptors,
like humans and wildlife.

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:43.310
And sometimes we have
to go back and forth

00:12:43.310 --> 00:12:47.300
with regulatory agencies to
define our conceptual model

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:50.570
for a site, which means we
might be adding in receptors,

00:12:50.570 --> 00:12:52.550
we might be adding
in compounds, we

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:56.160
might be changing how we
spatially divide up our data.

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:57.830
And that means we
very rarely have

00:12:57.830 --> 00:13:02.310
to just run ProUCL once,
usually two or three times.

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:04.450
So automating it
definitely saved time.

00:13:04.450 --> 00:13:07.620
It saved human error because
less copying and pasting.

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:10.440
But it's not perfect.

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:12.010
Once, I'm sure,

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:16.020
one day, hopefully EPA
will update ProUCL

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:18.040
and then my code
will become obsolete.

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:20.250
But at this point, it's
already paid for itself.

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:20.800
It works.

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:23.940
It does what we need it to
do, and we're happy for that.

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:25.440
In addition, there
are other things

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:28.470
I wanted to do to help
improve this function,

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:32.250
to make it easier to
use for anybody.

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:33.970
But we just didn't
have the time.

00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:36.030
So it does require some
special setup, especially

00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:39.000
with the coordinates for
the clicking of the mouse.

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:41.070
You have to make sure
your screen resolution is

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:44.110
set appropriately.

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:46.280
So final thoughts.

00:13:46.280 --> 00:13:49.360
I gave you a bunch of different
hybrid approaches to automation

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:52.900
that helped me save time and
reduce human error, which

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:55.480
ultimately is what I believe
the goal of automation

00:13:55.480 --> 00:13:57.220
is and should be.

00:13:57.220 --> 00:14:00.070
Also, I showed some easier
examples and some more

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:02.380
advanced, so I hope
you recognize that

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:06.130
no matter what your skill set,
you can benefit your team now.

00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:10.240
And maybe with just a little
bit of rephrasing of your pitch,

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:12.460
starting small and
being creative,

00:14:12.460 --> 00:14:13.540
you too can help.

00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:15.100
You can reduce the
activation energy

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:18.070
needed to get your team on
board and reduce the road

00:14:18.070 --> 00:14:20.370
bumps along the way.

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:24.450
And with that, if we have, I can
go ahead and answer questions.

00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:25.950
Also, I wanted to
let everybody know

00:14:25.950 --> 00:14:29.070
at this link are my slides
as well as example code

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:30.730
for the smaller examples.

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:34.530
So the open xlsx and
the officer as well as links

00:14:34.530 --> 00:14:37.160
to more information.

